<content><![CDATA[<p>Check your understanding of this lesson. Your answers aren't recorded.</p>
<multiple-choice question="What is texel density?" single-option="" randomize="">
  <input type="checkbox" value="The number of texture pixels per unit of surface on a model" data-correct="true">
  <input type="checkbox" value="The number of polygons per object">
  <input type="checkbox" value="The file size of a texture">
  <input type="checkbox" value="The number of materials in a scene">
</multiple-choice>
<true-false-question question="Combining several objects' textures into one atlas with a shared material can reduce draw calls.">
  <input type="checkbox" value="True" data-correct="true">
  <input type="checkbox" value="False">
</true-false-question>
<self-check title="Think it through">
  <p slot="question">Why do real-time assets need tighter budgets than assets for an offline render?</p>
  <p>A game has to draw each frame in a few milliseconds (about 16 ms at 60 fps) on players' hardware with limited memory, while an offline render can spend minutes per frame on a render farm. So polygon counts, texture memory and draw calls all have to be limited.</p>
</self-check>
